# crav1 kit

Specs, not chat history, are the source of truth. Put change work under `docs/specs/<change-id>/`. The layout always includes `docs/system/`. Spark and ideas seed a thin landscape when that folder is missing, including `glossary.md`, and do not rewrite an existing one. They fill a missing `glossary.md` when the folder already exists, and append only new rows when the file exists. Intake writes the landscape, including `glossary.md`, and does not skip it. Existing glossary rows are not rewritten. A meaning the source does not state is `to be researched`.

Type `/crav1` to list kit skills. Typical order: spark, ideas, or intake → feature branch (`feat/<slug>` or specify-only `spec/<slug>`) → architecture review → tighten → resolve questions → plan → review-plan → tighten-plan → implement (or `/crav1-complete-task` / `/crav1-complete-tasks` / `/crav1-complete-features`) → verify → fix-from-verify (do not edit `spec.md` in the fix loop). `/crav1-plan-from-spec` names whether this repo has a linter or checker for the code the tasks will touch. When it has one, the plan names it and Build is expected to leave that check green. When it does not, the plan says so and stops before Build. The user decides to add the linter or to go on without one. Do not install one from the plan skill. Specify does not name the linter. `/crav1-spark-to-spec` is greenfield, a brownfield feature, or a later feature on `docs/system/` (new slug unless they extend). It seeds a thin `docs/system/` when that folder is missing, including `glossary.md`, then the spec, then one index row. `/crav1-intake-to-specs` writes the landscape, including `glossary.md`, plus one spec per v0 feature. `/crav1-match-to-specs` matches existing repos plus a dump to the landscape, including `glossary.md`, and one spec per confirmed slice. It does not replace spark, ideas, or intake. `/crav1-match-dump-to-specs` sorts a later dump onto specs that already exist. It does not create a slug. It does not seed `docs/system/`. A missing `glossary.md` is filled only when that folder already exists, and new rows are appended when the file exists. `/crav1-code-into-specs` sorts a code change that already landed onto specs that already exist. No dump is required. It does not create a slug. It does not watch the repo. It does not seed `docs/system/`. A missing `glossary.md` is filled only when that folder already exists, and new rows are appended when the file exists. `/crav1-add-to-spec` adds new information to one existing spec and reports impact on the landscape or other specs before editing them. It does not create a glossary. A new glossary row can be one listed edit. `/crav1-security-review` is cross-cutting. It runs only when architecture already exists (`docs/system/` or a spec that already describes the design). The same command covers the whole system, one existing spec, or the change in front of us. It writes kept findings and stops. It does not plan, implement, or commit. Spark, plan, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-review-pr` is cross-cutting. It runs only when the user names an open pull request in that turn, or clearly asks to review that pull request. It reads the diff and names the lane for a finding. It does not edit code, the spec, or the plan, and it does not vote, comment, install a linter, or merge. Spark, specify, and verify do not run it. `/crav1-open-pr` may name it and does not run it. `/crav1-merge-pr` does not run it. A review is not a merge ask.

Do not scaffold an application unless the user asked for one. Prefer editing the spec or plan when implementation drifts.

Full install paths: `docs/install.md` in the kit repo. After a plugin install, start with the plugin README.
